# Redox Tuning in Crystalline and Electronic Structure of Bimetal–Organic Frameworks Derived Cobalt/Nickel Boride/Sulfide for Boosted Faradaic Capacitance

## abstract
The development of efficient electrode materials is a cutting-edge approach for high-performance energy storage devices. Herein, an effective chemical redox approach is reported for tuning the crystalline and electronic structures of bimetallic cobalt/nickel-organic frameworks (Co-Ni MOFs) to boost faradaic redox reaction for high energy density. The as-obtained cobalt/nickel boride/sulfide exhibits a high specific capacitance (1281 F g-1 at 1 A g-1 ), remarkable rate performance (802.9 F g-1 at 20 A g-1 ), and outstanding cycling stability (92.1% retention after 10 000 cycles). An energy storage device fabricated with a cobalt/nickel boride/sulfide electrode exhibits a high energy density of 50.0 Wh kg-1 at a power density of 857.7 W kg-1 , and capacity retention of 87.7% (up to 5000 cycles at 12 A g-1 ). Such an effective redox approach realizes the systematic electronic tuning that activates the fast faradaic reactions of the metal species in cobalt/nickel boride/sulfide which may shed substantial light on inspiring MOFs and their derivatives for energy storage devices.

The development of efficient electrode materials is a cutting-edge approach for high-performance energy storage devices. Herein, an effective chemical redox approach is reported for tuning the crystalline and electronic structures of bimetallic cobalt/nickel–organic frameworks (Co-Ni MOFs) to boost faradaic redox reaction for high energy density. The as-obtained cobalt/nickel boride/ sulfide exhibits a high specific capacitance $\lvert128\rvert\textsf{F g}^{-1}$ at $\mathsf{1}\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\mathsf{I}$ , remarkable rate performance $1802.9\textsf{F}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at $20\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\bigr]$ ), and outstanding cycling stability $(92.7\%$ retention after 10 000 cycles). An energy storage device fabricated with a cobalt/nickel boride/sulfide electrode exhibits a high energy density of $50.0~\mathsf{W h\ k g^{-1}}$ at a power density of $857.7\ \mathsf{W}\ \mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ , and capacity retention of $87.7\%$ (up to 5000 cycles at $\mathbf{12Ag^{-1}}$ ). Such an effective redox approach realizes the systematic electronic tuning that activates the fast faradaic reactions of the metal species in cobalt/nickel boride/sulfide which may shed substantial light on inspiring MOFs and their derivatives for energy storage devices.
Extensive energy demands by portable electronics and electric vehicles have indorsed the rapid development of highly efficient energy storage technologies for human society.[1–4]
Electrochemical capacitors (ECs) have received significant attention as a strong candidate in virtue of high power density, fast charging rate, and long-term cyclability.[2,5–9] Since the performance metrics and energy storage mechanism of ECs depend on the electrode materials, extensive investigations have been performed to optimize the structure and electronic valence of inorganic materials to improve the energy density.[10]
Recently, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), which are self-assembled by metal ions (or metal clusters) with organic ligands in high crystallinity and longrange order, are considered as emerging porous materials for electrochemical energy storage applications.[11–15] In sharp contrast to the inorganic electrode materials, their diverse topology structure, tunable porosity, and abundant metal ions allow them to be a promising energy storage material.[16,17] However, the poor conductivity and lack of structural stability of pristine MOFs remain the critical limitations for practical applications in energy storage devices.[18,19] Except for MOFs-derived nanocarbons or metal compounds,[15,20,21] hybridizing MOFs with conductive polymers or carbon substrates is an additional productive tactic to improve the conductivity and stability, which can accelerate the electron transfer and reduce the inner resistance during the electrochemical reactions, even though the loading of active MOF species is lowered by the existence of conducting materials in a hybrid composition.[22–25] Nevertheless, the charge storage behaviors of these MOFs materials are mainly based on the surface area-dependent and non-faradaic electrical double-layer capacitance (EDLC), while there is a poor utilization of abundant redox-active metal ion sites at the higher rates.[26] Therefore, the conductivity and structural integrity of MOF itself should be improved, while the redox-active metal species need to be activated for boosting fast and reversible faradaic reactions.[27]
In this work, we report a chemical redox approach by tuning the crystalline and electronic structure of bimetallic Co-Ni MOFs to reveal the facile and reversible faradaic behaviors for the greatly enhanced EC performance. This approach is carried out by activating the conductivity and electrochemical performance of the bimetallic Co-Ni MOF with sodium borohydride firstly. Importantly, the initial nanosheet structure remains intact, whereas the valence state of redox-active $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Ni}$ species is lowered, thereby achieving the outstanding conductivity and capacitance. In the subsequent sulfurization process, the oxidized boron receives electrons and the sulfur anions lose the electrons simultaneously, the resultant boron and sulfur species will consequently interact with the metallic cobalt and nickel to form a relatively stable low-valence mixed state. This chemical approach is capable to achieve the unprecedented charge storage performance of cobalt/nickel boride/sulfide (Co-Ni-B-S), such as a high specific capacitance, rate performance, and long cycling stability. Taking full potential of the asdesigned MOFs derivatives, asymmetric ECs are configured by integrating with conventional activated carbon as the counter electrode to achieve high energy and power densities.
The Co-Ni-B-S derived from bimetallic MOFs is prepared through a simple three steps process, beginning with the preparation of Co-Ni MOFs by a hydrothermal method followed by the boronization (Co-Ni-B) and subsequent sulfurization (Co-Ni-B-S), as shown in Figure 1. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) and galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD) curves of Co-Ni MOF with various $\mathrm{{Co}/\mathrm{{Ni}}}$ ratios are measured to analyze the optimum stoichiometry of the MOF microspheres with the highest capacitance (Figure S1a,b, Supporting Information). X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns indicate that the intensity of the peaks located at $11.6^{\circ}$ and $16.8^{\circ}$ increase gradually with the reduced $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Ni}$ ratio from 8:1 to 1:4 and slightly decrease with $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Ni}$ ratio reducing to 1:8 (Figure S1c, Supporting Information). The Co-Ni MOF with the 1:4 ratio $(\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Ni})$ exhibits the highest peak intensity, suggesting the capacitance reliance on the structural change. The MOFs with different $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Ni}$ ratios are activated by boronization and sulfurization in order to find the optimum composition (Figure S2a,b, Supporting Information). As observed from the CV and GCD curves, Co-Ni-B-S with $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Ni}$ ratio of 1:4 demo­ nstrates the maximum specific capacity, which indicates that the Co/Ni ratio 1:4 is the most appropriate proportion for both the initial MOFs and Co-Ni-B-S products. Hence, the following experiments are carried out on Co-Ni MOFs with an optimized Co/Ni ratio of 1:4 (Figure S1d, Supporting Information).
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of the Co-NiB-S show that the nanosheet assembly morphology remains intact with a comparable size of ${\approx}5~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ even after the redox treatment of boronization and sulfurization (Figure  2a;
Figure S3a,b, Supporting Information). The sheet-stacked structure of the precursors remains intact after the redox reactions (Figure  2b), while a large number of small-sized nanosheets are in situ formed on the surface (Figure  2c). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images further confirm the ultrathin and wrinkled nanosheets structure in the Co-Ni-B-S even after the harsh redox reactions (Figure  2d; Figure S3c, Supporting Information). The open nanostructures would be favorable for exposing more redox-active sites on the surface.[28,29] The selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern in Figure 2e indicates the as-obtained Co-Ni-BS is amorphous. However, the internal region of the wrinkles shows visible lattice fringes (Figure  2f), indicating the existence of crystalline domain corresponding to the folded parts. The more distinct image reveals the presence of lattice fringe surrounded by the amorphous structure outward (Figure S3d, Supporting Information). Such a unique combination of partial crystalline and amorphous structures in the porous Co-NiB-S might be one of the reasons to accomplish high energy density.
For more in-depth understandings of the formation mechanism at the Co-Ni-B-S structure, the samples treated with $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ (Co-Ni-B), $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ (Co-Ni-S), and $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ followed by $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ (Co-Ni-S-B) are also investigated (Figure  3). The initial Co-Ni MOF shows a hierarchical structure with ${\approx}5~{\upmu}\mathrm{m}$ microsphere containing interconnected nanosheets with a thickness of $3{-}5\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  3a, Figure $S4\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-c},$ Supporting Information). TEM image confirms the nanosheet assembled structure of initial Co-Ni MOF (Figure  3e). Some disordered structure can be seen in the high-resolution (HR)TEM image (Figure  3i). After the boronization, the as-formed Co-Ni-B exhibits the smaller size of wrinkled nanosheets (Figure 3b,f; Figure S4d–f, Supporting Information). TEM images of Co-Ni-B illustrate complex and distinct lattice fringes as a polycrystalline characteristic with the short-range ordered and long-range disordered state (Figure  3j; Figure S5a, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, inverse fast Fourier transform (FFT) images are collected for Co-Ni-B and Co-Ni-B-S (Figure S5a,b, Supporting Information), where the messy and distorted lattice fringes are clearly seen. More importantly, a large number of black zones could be observed between the distorted lattices, which are formed during the boronization process, having the irregular lattice defects, with the lattice spacing of $0.204\ \mathrm{nm}$ . After treatment with $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , the lattice fringes are distorted in a homogenous way and the black zones disappear, while the similar lattice spacing of $0.204~\mathrm{nm}$ remains intact. These results indicate that the sulfurization of Co-Ni-B does not significantly change the crystalline state of the material. As demonstrated by the uniform doping of sulfur revealed by the elemental mapping (Figure S6, Supporting Information), sulfur has been successfully incorporated into the vacancies.[30] However, after the direct sulfurization of Co-Ni MOF, the Co-Ni-S and Co-Ni-S-B samples show the condensation trend (Figure 3c,d; Figures $\mathrm{S4g-l}$ , Supporting Information), where the nanosheets disappear with a severe aggregation (Figure  3g,h). It signifies the sulfurization treatment by $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ is a dominant attack, which would destroy the whole nanostructure of Co-Ni MOF (Figure 3k,l). Moreover, this redox treatment also induces an optimized porous structure. As shown in the nitrogen sorption isotherm curves (Figure S7, Supporting Information), the initial MOF shows a relatively low specific surface area of $61.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . After the boronization, the as-obtained Co-Ni-B shows an enhanced specific area of about $106~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , since a large amount of smaller sized nanosheets are in situ formed and stacked together to provide additional surface area. Moreover, this stacking leads to the reduction of pore size of Co-Ni-B to $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ , smaller than $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ of initial Co-Ni MOF. Furthermore, the specific surface area of Co-NiB-S slightly increase to $116.3~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , revealing that the sulfurization does not demolish the porous structure of Co-Ni-B on the macro level. Simultaneously, a large number of micropores are produced due to the formation of the additional surface arising from the smaller nanosheet of Co-Ni-B-S by excess $S^{2-}$ . Mesoporous associated with microporous architecture with a large specific surface area would benefit for the electrolyte penetration, facilitate the short ion diffusion pathways and lead to higher energy storage. Therefore, the efficient exploitation of the redox-active sites exposed on the surface could promote the electrochemical performance of Co-Ni-B-S.[26,29,31]
The redox treatment of Co-Ni MOFs induces the modifications in the electronic and microstructure rather than the elemental distribution, which is evidenced by the uniform element dispersion in the Co-Ni-B-S materials (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Moreover, the atomic ratio of Co/Ni (1:4) is preserved after the redox reactions, implying the potentially good electrochemical properties of Co-Ni-B-S for the unchanged content ratio of Co/Ni. The structural evolution of Co-Ni MOFs with different redox treatments are investigated by XRD technique. Compared to the initial MOFs, the diffraction peak appearance at ${\approx}35^{\circ}$ indicates the partial formation of metallic Co/Ni species after the boronization process (Figure  4a), which is consistent with the HRTEM images. By contrast, no distinct diffraction peak is observed after the sulfurization, which verifies the irreversibility of the boronization and sulfurization sequence in the evolution of an amorphous structure. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is further carried out to investigate the detailed surface electronic states (Figure S8a, Supporting Information). The peaks for $\mathrm{Co}2\mathrm{p}3/2$ and Ni 2p3/2 first shift negatively and then positively with the treatment of boronization followed by sulfurization (Figure  4b,c), which indicates a valence change caused by the activation process. Hence, the first reduction by $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ treatment results in the formation of the metallic Co and Ni species, which are enriched with electrons and responsible for the negative peaks shift. The positive shifts in binding energies of Co and Ni 2p3/2 peaks suggest the enhanced oxidation ability of the Co-Ni-B-S sample. Some low-valence $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Ni}$ gets oxidized to more stable and dominant $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , which are essential for the electrochemical performance, while the $\mathrm{Co}^{3+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ on the surface could be the inactive species for electrochemical reaction.[32]
In Figure  4d, the peaks at 192.9 and $191.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ for Co-Ni-B indicate the existence of oxidized boron, which implies that the valence of boron is maintained. In the sample Co-Ni-B-S, the peak at $186.98\ \mathrm{eV}$ indicates that the oxidized boron is reduced by $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ . The boron atom tends to occupy the lattice and combine with the metals due to its small atomic size. The formation of such a complex metal-B structure has excellent oxidation resistance, which would significantly benefit the electrochemical properties. The peaks at 163.28 and $162.08\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{~S~}2\mathrm{p}$ are related to the metal-S bonds (Figure 4e).[33] The peaks at 168.7 and $168.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{~S~}2\mathrm{p}$ are the intimations of $s{\mathrm{-}}0$ bonds, suggesting the efficient filling of S in the vacancies.[20,30]Moreover, the O 1s XPS profiles of Co-Ni MOF and Co-Ni-B-S can be deconvoluted into three distinct peaks (Figure  4f), which can be classified as lattice oxygen $(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{I}},\approx531.1~\mathrm{eV})$ ), oxygen vacancies $(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{II}},\approx531.8\ \mathrm{eV})$ , and chemisorbed or dissociated oxygen or OH species $(\mathrm{O_{III}},\approx533.4~\mathrm{eV})$ .[34] Compared with the Co-Ni MOF, the $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{II}}$ peak intensity in the Co-Ni-B-S sample increases significantly, which implies the introduction of oxygen vacancies in the redox process.[35] The loss of oxygen could produce a large number of positive holes and defects, which are beneficial for the electrons transfer. With the redox treatment, the oxygen vacancies are formed and the $\mathsf{S}^{2-}$ is absorbed by the positive holes through the electrostatic attraction. All above will be responsible for the formation of defect structure, which is consistent with the HRTEM observation (Figure  3).[36] The spectra of Co-Ni-S and Co-Ni-S-B are also analyzed comparatively (Figure S8, Supporting Information). The peaks for Co and Ni are positively shifted, which indicates that the Co and Ni ions lose electrons and become oxidized (Figure S8b,c, Supporting Information). Besides, sulfurization is considered a violent reaction process for the metal ions and $S^{2-}$ to form metal-S bonds, which would stabilize of Co and Ni species and strengthen their antioxidizing ability.[37] Although the reduction is carried out subsequently using sodium borohydride, the collapsed structure of Co-Ni-S could not be reversed into welldeveloped sheet structure (Figure 3). This collapsed nanosheet would restrict the electrochemical performance of Co-Ni-S and Co-Ni-S-B.
The potential applications of the Co-Ni MOF and its derivatives in energy storage are evaluated by CV at different scan rates with the potential window $-0.1$ to $0.65\mathrm{~V~}$ versus $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ (Figure S9, Supporting Information). According to the CV profiles of Co-Ni-B-S at different scan rates (Figure  5a), the current densities of redox peaks increase with the increased scan rates indicating a relatively low resistance of electrode material and fast redox reactions at the electrode/electrolyte interface. Meanwhile, the Co-Ni-B-S electrode also exhibits a closely rectangular shape with two pairs of redox peaks, which suggests a facile and reversible charge–discharge response. However, the CV curves are not amplified proportionally, which is attributed to the absorption peaks with slow scan rates from the porous sheet stacked structure (Figure  5b). The Co-Ni-B-S presents the highest capacitance with two pairs of redox peaks at the scan rate of $20\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ , whereas only one pair for the other materials (Co-Ni MOF, Co-Ni-B, Co-Ni-S, and Co-Ni-S-B) is resulted from the ${\mathrm{Co}}/{\mathrm{Ni}}$ multivalences and amorphous structure after the redox treatment.[38,39] The electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) of Co-Ni MOF and its derivatives are also evaluated through the CV measurements at the potential window $_{(0-0.1~\mathrm{V}}$ versus $\mathrm{Hg/HgO)}$ (Figure S10, Supporting Information).[8,9,19] The specific electrical double layer capacitance $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ of Co-Ni MOF, Co-Ni-B, Co-Ni-B-S, Co-Ni-S, and Co-Ni-S-B is 110.66, 45.8, 145.4, 48, and $2.5\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ , respectively. It can be concluded that the ECSA of Co-Ni-B-S is much larger than that of the initial Co-Ni MOF electrode, suggesting their enhanced electrochemical performance in aqueous electro­ lyte, and authorizing the superiority of this structure. The total stored charge of an electrode can be divided into surface capacitive- and diffusion-controlled contributions.[40] The surface capacitive-controlled process includes the fast ions adsorption/ desorption EDLC and fast faradaic reactions of redox species, whereas the latter process is controlled by the diffusion of the ions between the electrode material and electrolyte. Figure  5c illustrates the contributions from the ion-diffusion and capacitive process of Co-Ni-B-S at the scan rate of $5~\mathrm{mV~s^{-1}}$ . Compared with the contribution curve (Figure S11a, Supporting Information) of Co-Ni MOF, both diffusion and capacitive contributions of Co-Ni-B-S are increased, which indicates that the electronic tuning by the redox treatment could enhance the ion diffusion abilities and capacitance simultaneously. For a better understanding of the enhanced performance of Co-Ni-B-S, the capacitive- and diffusion-controlled contributions in the samples of Co-Ni MOF, Co-Ni-B, Co-Ni-S, and Co-Ni-B-S are also calculated (Figure S11, Supporting Information).[35,41] It is evident that the increased scan rates enhance the proportion of capacitive contribution while the diffusion contribution proportion decrease, which is further proved by the irregular growth of CV curves (Figure S12, Supporting Information). It can be observed that the capacitive-controlled contribution in Co-Ni-B reveals the lowest proportion, which might be produced by the formation of Co-Ni-B alloys and Co-Ni hydroxides species with battery behavior.[38,42] Meanwhile, the highest capacitivecontrolled contribution in Co-Ni-B-S demonstrates the larger surface area for fast EDLC process and ion-diffusion ability, which is benefited from the reduced resistance by the electronic tuning process.
The GCD tests are also performed to explore the electrochemical behaviors of Co-Ni MOF and its derivatives. As depicted in Figure  5d, a series of symmetrical GCD curves reflect the good electrochemical reversibility of Co-Ni-B-S. An excellent rate capability of $93.4\%$ capacity retention is achieved from 1 A $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ $(1281.2\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1})$ to $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ $(1196.4\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1})$ . Contrary to the other as-prepared electrode materials, Co-Ni-B-S exhibits the highest capacitance and the most symmetric GCD curve (Figure  5e), suggesting an excellent capacitive property, superior reversibility and rate capability for the faradaic redox reactions (Figure S13, Supporting Information). Compared with similar MOFs and their derivatives, the obtained Co-NiB-S exhibits superior performance (Table S1, Supporting Information), identifying the feasibility of the redox treatments with structure and electronic tuning. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is further evaluated to better understand the material properties in the electrolyte (Figure S14,
Supporting Information). The EIS spectrum is composed of a semicircle part in the high-frequency region and a linear component in the low-frequency region. The equivalent circuit mainly includes the elements: the equivalent series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ , charge-transfer resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ , the constant phase angle element (CPE), and Warburg resistance (W).[43] In the highfrequency region, the intercept value on the $x$ -coordinate corresponds to the solution resistance $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ , and the diameter of the semicircle corresponds to the reaction resistance $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ of faradaic reactions and EDLC. Among them, Co-Ni-B-S shows a little resistance, which is concordant for its excellent electrochemical performance (Figure S14a, Supporting Information). The lowfrequency region could be attributed to the charge transfer process at the electrode-electrolyte interface and the electrolyte ion diffusion. The high slope of Co-Ni-B-S shows the low diffusion resistance, which promotes the intercalation effect for higher energy capacity from ion diffusion. The Co-Ni-B-S electrode indicates the lowest $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ of $0.09\Omega$ , compared to initial Co-Ni MOF (0.61 Ω), Co-Ni-B $\left(0.14~\Omega\right)$ , Co-Ni-S $\left(0.18~\Omega\right)$ , and Co-Ni-S-B $\left(0.11~\Omega\right)$ electrodes (Table S2, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, it can be seen that the $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ decrease with the boronization treatment, which suggests a better charge transfer kinetics of the Co-Ni-S-B materials. In the low-frequency region, the closer to $90^{\circ}$ perpendicular to the real axis means good ions diffusion ability, which is highly consistent with the energy capacity contribution from ion diffusion ability. The EIS results also indicate the superior electrical conductivity and electroactivity of Co-Ni-B-S, which is mainly due to the stable porous and amorphous structure created by the redox reactions. Moreover, Co-Ni-B-S exhibits a remarkable cycling performance with capacitance retention of $92.1\%$ after $10\ 000$ cycles at the current density of $15\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ (Figure  5f), which would contribute to significant advantages for fast-charging energy storage devices. Moreover, SEM images of the cycled Co-Ni-B-S sample shows the remained intact nanosheets (Figure S15, Supporting Information). The stacked structure can still ensure the sufficient penetration of electrolyte, which would be the reason for maintaining the structure at a high current density.
The above results demonstrate that Co-Ni-B-S is a promising electrode to improve the energy density of ECs. By constructing an asymmetric full-cell device with commercial EDLC electrode material of activated carbon (AC), we could demonstrate the superiority of our Co-Ni-B-S sample for high-performance device. Therefore, an asymmetric fast-charge device is assembled by employing the as-synthesized Co-Ni-B-S/nickel foam (NF) as the positive electrode and AC/NF as the negative electrode, respectively (Figure  6a). Comparing the CV profiles of the Co-Ni-B-S electrode and AC/NF electrode at a scan rate of $20\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ (Figure S16, Supporting Information), the respective working potential ranges are $-0.1$ to $0.65\mathrm{~V~}$ and $-1.0$ to $0\mathrm{\:V.}$ The AC/NF negative electrode CV displays a similar rectangular shape, suggesting the ideal double-layer capacitor behavior based on fast diffusion/adsorption of electrolyte ions. A series of CV files of the Co-Ni-B-S//AC are shown with operation voltages varying from $0{-}1.4\mathrm{~V~}$ to $0{-}1.7\mathrm{~V~}$ at a sweep rate of $10\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ (Figure  6b). This device could extend to a large voltage window of $1.7~\mathrm{V}$ stably. The presence of redox peaks in all curves implies the battery-like property due to the faradaic reaction of metal species. CV curves of the device at the sweep rates of 5, 10, 20, and $50~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ in Figure 6c and GCD curves at the area current densities of 3, 5, 8, 10, 20, 40, 80, and $160\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-1}}$ in Figure 6d at the potential window of $_{0-1.7\mathrm{~V~}}$ declare a hybrid energy storage behavior including both batterytype and electrical double-layer capacitive characteristics. From the GCD curves in Figure 6d, the volumetric capacitance of the prepared device is 1203, 1179.7, 1165.7, 1110.2, 1009.2, 960.5, 937.4, and $914~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ at the current densities of 3, 5, 8, 10, 20, 40, 80, and $160\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , respectively. The specific capacitance is calculated to be $112\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . The device shows a low impedance $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ of $0.14\Omega$ , which could facilitate the electron transfer and reduce the internal energy dissipation (Figure S17, Supporting Information). Correspondingly, the energy densities and the power densities of the prepared device are figured out in Figure  6e. The designed device can deliver an energy density of $45.0~\mathrm{Wh~kg^{-1}}$ at the power density of $857.7~\mathrm{W}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . Even after the power density increased to $13.6\ensuremath{~\mathrm{KW~}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}}$ , the device still demonstrates an energy density of 36.8 Wh $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ , outperforming most of the excellent energy storage devices (Figure 6e), such as nickel-cobalt phosphate//AC,[44] NiNTAS $@$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}//\mathrm{NiNTAS@MnO}_{2}$ ,[45] $\mathrm{Ni_{0.55}C o_{0.45}C_{2}O_{4}//A C,}$ [46] Ni-Co $\mathrm{LDH}@\mathrm{NF}//\mathrm{NiCoO}_{2}@\mathrm{NF},$ [47] CoNi-MOF//AC,[38] and $\mathsf{C C@}$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}A l.L D H//C C@Z P C.^{\left[5\right.}}$ ] Moreover, the asymmetric device shows excellent cycling stability with retention of $87.7\%$ at a current density of $12\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ after 5000 cycles (Figure  6f), suggesting the robust interconnected networks and reasonable strain accommodation of the hybrid electrode.
In summary, this work demonstrates an effective redox approach by tuning the crystalline and electronic structure of the Co-Ni-MOF-derived Co-Ni-B-S to boost their fast-faradaic behaviors and achieve enhanced electrochemical energy storage performance. The as-activated Co-Ni-B-S exhibits a high specific capacitance $(1281\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1})$ , remarkable rate performance $(802.9\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at $20\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ), and outstanding cycling stability $(92.1\%$ retention after $10\ 000$ cycles). An energy storage device fabricated by this activated Co-Ni-B-S exhibits an energy density of $50.0\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of 857.7 W $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ with the capacity retention of $87.7\%$ (up to 5000 cycles at $12\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}};$ . We anticipate that the current approach would provide valuable insights in the electronic structure regulation of MOFs for energy storage technologies and beyond.
